SUMMARY:[現場] Applied Physics/Physics Colloquium: Jo Dunkley - "The Simo
 ns Observatory"
LOCATION:Hewlett Teaching Center\, 201
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-jo-
 dunkley-the-simons-observatory
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/applied-physicsphysics-colloq
 uium-jo-dunkley-the-simons-observatory\n\nAbstract: The Simons Observatory
 \, a new millimeter-wave observatory in the Atacama\, is now gathering dat
 a. With its smaller telescopes we are seeking a primordial signal imprinte
 d by gravitational waves generated in the early universe. Its largest tele
 scope is measuring the finer scales of the CMB with increased sensitivity\
 , probing the initial conditions of the early universe as well as revealin
 g the later-formed cosmic web of gas and dark matter through the gravitati
 onal lensing and Sunyaev Zel’dovich effects. It will map half the sky at
  arcminute resolution\, overlapping with a number of large-scale structure
  surveys including Rubin. By surveying half the sky every couple of days\,
  we also hope to see new types of transient astronomical events in millime
 ter-wavelengths.  I will talk about the current status of SO\, and its sci
 ence plans.\n\nJo Dunkley is the Joseph Henry Professor of Physics and Ast
 rophysical Sciences at Princeton University. She works on the analysis and
  interpretation of cosmological observations. She is currently the Spokesp
 erson for the Simons Observatory collaboration. Previously she was a membe
 r of NASA's WMAP science team and a scientist on the European Space Agency
 ’s Planck satellite project\, and was the analysis leader for the Atacam
 a Cosmology Telescope. She is a Fellow of the Royal Society.
END:VEVENT
